WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.779
A secure hashing roadmap begins with purpose.

2
00:00:02.779 --> 00:00:08.336
Define what the hash is expected to prove before choosing an algorithm or format.

3
00:00:08.336 --> 00:00:16.672
Is it a file fingerprint, an integrity check, a password verifier, an authenticated tag, a cache key, or a compatibility marker?

4
00:00:16.672 --> 00:00:21.832
Each answer points to different security properties, libraries, storage formats, and review questions.

5
00:00:21.832 --> 00:00:27.786
Writing that purpose down is small work, but it prevents years of ambiguous helper reuse.

6
00:00:27.786 --> 00:00:30.962
Use approved algorithms, platform APIs, and vetted libraries.

7
00:00:30.962 --> 00:00:34.932
Keep wrappers thin, documented, and specific to the intended construction.

8
00:00:34.932 --> 00:00:38.504
Do not use general-purpose hashes for password storage.

9
00:00:38.504 --> 00:00:46.840
Use password hashing or key-derivation mechanisms for password verifiers, and use HMAC or another approved authentication mechanism when origin and

10
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:48.031
integrity both matter.

11
00:00:48.031 --> 00:00:53.588
Canonicalize inputs before hashing so the digest covers the exact bytes the design intends.

12
00:00:53.588 --> 00:00:58.352
Stream large data safely, check read errors, and define what is covered.

13
00:00:58.352 --> 00:01:05.894
Store digest metadata clearly, encode consistently, avoid accidental truncation, and compare digests with appropriate validation and timing-safe APIs

14
00:01:05.894 --> 00:01:06.687
when needed.

15
00:01:06.687 --> 00:01:09.863
Plan migration away from weak or legacy algorithms.

16
00:01:09.863 --> 00:01:13.833
Test boundary cases, old formats, invalid values, and failure paths.

17
00:01:13.833 --> 00:01:20.184
Review code, dependencies, stored formats, and configuration together because hashing decisions often span all of them.

18
00:01:20.184 --> 00:01:23.756
Make ownership of those decisions explicit, documented, and reviewable.

19
00:01:23.756 --> 00:01:27.329
Secure hashing is not just calling a hash function.

20
00:01:27.329 --> 00:01:33.680
It is choosing the right construction for the security goal and governing that choice over time.
